From: Ferestre contributors
Subject: Keep accounts consistent and the service up

The first account added on a fresh install becomes the default, under the
same lock migration uses, so a launch or `account` that is not told which
account to use no longer falls back to the empty legacy keychain and fails
as signed out. Selection, in the client and in the service, uses the legacy
unscoped sign-in only while no profile exists at all: a lone profile without
a default (as an earlier Add account left it) is adopted, and several with
none chosen are an error that says so. Migration with no legacy sign-in also
adopts a lone profile.

Add account removes the new sign-in's credentials on every failure,
including a panic, until its profile is saved. The Xbox identity step
reports errors instead of panicking: `xsts_for` uses the reporting XSTS
call, and a token without a user claim is an error rather than a panic when
building its Authorization header.

Signing out a profile also removes the legacy, pre-profile entries when they
belong to the same Microsoft account, so a migrated account's refresh
credential does not stay in the keyring and cannot be revived by a later
migration. Device registration is untouched.

Neither HTTP client is built with connection_verbose any more: at trace
level it logged the decrypted traffic, Authorization headers and token
bodies included. The service shares one client across connections. A failed
accept() is logged and retried after a pause instead of ending the service.

Catalog queries upper-case Store IDs, so a title that passes them in lower
case matches the catalog's answer. An XSTS request may carry
<ForceRefresh>true</ForceRefresh>, the title's ForceRefresh option, and then
skips the cached token; it re-mints at most once per 30 s per token key.
Older runtimes do not send it and older services ignore it.

The sign-in window accepts bridge messages only from login.live.com and
login.microsoftonline.com pages, navigates only to https Microsoft account
domains (and the challenge provider their pages embed), and passes the
page-supplied context to its script as a JSON value. The plain-file
credential store (key-chain-file) refuses to work without HOME instead of
using the shared temporary directory, and keeps its file 0600 in a
directory only the user can write. xbl-check shortens a response line on a
character boundary.

